import json
A = {}
A["stage1/problem/A1/v2"] = """Problem: Can MVFA's segmentation adapters learn transferable localisation from image-level labels alone? Today they learn only from pixel masks of BrainMRI, LiverCT and RESC. We replace the Dice and Focal seg_loss with a multiple-instance objective applied at all four levels to the segmentation-branch patch-to-text similarity map (mean of the top 2% patch anomaly probabilities; max and mean pooling as ablations), and train on all five source datasets with masks discarded. Primary question and hypothesis: because every patch is scored against a shared text anchor, image-level supervision aligns patch-level evidence with the text 'abnormal' direction, so the learned localisation transfers to the unseen modality and retains at least 80% of mask-supervised MVFA's pixel-level AUROC (with PRO and image AUROC also reported). A visual-only MIL control (same operator on a linear patch classifier without text anchors) tests whether text anchoring is responsible. Secondary analyses: per-level localisation quality, and a label-budget comparison on two held-out modalities between image-labelled training and mask-supervised training on 10%, 30% and 100% of the masked images.

Rationale: The final review asked to separate primary from secondary outcomes, to add a visual-only control that tests the text-anchoring hypothesis directly, and to limit the label-budget curve to fit one Colab GPU. All three are incorporated. The primary question is a single, falsifiable comparison on one component (the segmentation loss) of the public code, evaluated under the shared protocol with source-validation model selection."""
A["stage1/problem/A2/v2"] = """Problem: Is agreement across MVFA's four feature levels a better confidence signal than single-level confidence for source-free adaptation to a new modality? Pixels of an unlabelled target pool are pseudo-labelled only where all four zero-shot maps agree (lowest or highest 10% at every level, fixed in advance); both adapter sets are fine-tuned for three self-training rounds on confident pixels; the control uses deepest-layer confidence with the same pixel budget. Outcomes on a disjoint 50/50 test split, with bootstrap confidence intervals: image AUROC, pixel AUROC and PRO versus pool size N, pseudo-label precision against ground-truth masks, and the N at which few-shot MVFA with K = 4 labelled normal and abnormal images is matched.

Rationale: The reviewers' remaining requests — fix the quantile in advance and report confidence intervals — are incorporated; the problem remains a single comparison of confidence signals with a deployment-relevant secondary outcome."""
A["stage1/problem/B1/v2"] = """Problem: Can MVFA's few-shot fusion be made inductive — scoring each image on its own, without the test-set min-max normalisation of the released code — without losing AUROC, and does this give a threshold that transfers to unseen modalities? The inductive fusion standardises each branch with leave-one-out statistics of the K normal support images (each support's zero-shot map, and its few-shot map against a memory bank built from the other K-1 supports, pooled over patches and levels), adds the standardised branches, and pools the fused map with the mean of its top 2% pixels; the threshold is the 95th percentile of the supports' leave-one-out image scores. Primary outcome: image- and pixel-level AUROC of inductive versus transductive fusion on every held-out modality and K in {2, 4, 8, 16}. Motivating measurement: the spread of the transductive AUROC across random test subsets and at 10% and 50% prevalence. Secondary outcomes: sensitivity, realised specificity, F1 and expected calibration error at the support-derived threshold. We also record whether the few-shot baselines compared in the target paper (APRIL-GAN, WinCLIP) normalise over the test set in their public code, to judge how widely the finding applies.

Rationale: The final review found the problem fully specified and asked only to check whether common baselines share the transductive practice, which is added as a recorded side outcome. The problem remains confined to the scoring code of the target method and is inference-only apart from re-running the baseline under the shared protocol."""
A["stage1/problem/B2/v2"] = """Problem: In zero-shot deployment, does a false-positive guarantee calibrated on normal images of the source modalities hold on an unseen BMAD modality, and does label-free covariate-shift weighting repair it? Per-image scores are computed without test-set normalisation, with max, mean and top-2% aggregation. Split-conformal thresholds are calibrated on held-out normal source images at nominal false-positive rates of 1%, 5% and 10%. The weighted variant reweights calibration images by density ratios from a domain classifier between source calibration features and the unlabelled target batch, an explicit transductive-in-features, label-free assumption. Outcomes per held-out modality: realised false-positive rate with bootstrap intervals, coverage violation, sensitivity at the threshold, and the relation between violation size and source-target feature distance.

Rationale: The final review raised no further issues; the problem is unchanged apart from wording."""
A["stage1/problem/D1/v2"] = """Problem: Does tying a compact set of learnable normal prototypes to MVFA's normal text anchor improve zero-shot transfer? At each adapter level, M prototypes are initialised from the normal text embedding plus small noise and trained jointly with the adapters on the source modalities, with a compactness loss pulling normal patches to their nearest prototype and a margin (the 95th percentile of normal-patch distances on source validation data) keeping abnormal patches away; the anomaly map adds the prototype distance to the text-similarity map. Outcomes: zero-shot image AUROC, pixel AUROC and PRO on the held-out modality for M in {8, 32}, versus MVFA and versus untied (randomly initialised) prototypes.

Rationale: The final review raised no further issues; the problem is unchanged."""
A["stage1/problem/D2/v2"] = """Problem: Is the text side of MVFA a bottleneck, and does conditioning the normal anchor on the support normals help most on the modalities furthest from the source pool? First, the variance of zero- and few-shot AUROC over 20 random subsets of 5, 10 and 20 of the 35 templates is measured. Then the static normal anchor is shifted by a light projector applied to the mean adapted feature of the K support normals, with abnormal anchors matched many-to-one, and compared with test-image conditioning of the same projector. Hypothesis: support conditioning helps more than test-image conditioning, and most on modalities with the largest source-target feature distance. Outcomes: few-shot image AUROC, pixel AUROC and PRO per held-out modality, and template-sensitivity variance.

Rationale: The final review raised no further issues; the problem is unchanged."""
